WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.940
The basic consumer model starts with IDataProtectionProvider.

2
00:00:04.040 --> 00:00:10.280
Application code asks the provider to create an IDataProtector for a specific purpose.

3
00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:21.660
That protector can then protect a plaintext value into a protected payload and later unprotect that payload to validate it and recover the original value when the payload and keys are valid.

4
00:00:21.940 --> 00:00:24.900
Purpose strings are part of the security boundary.

5
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.440
They isolate different consumers even when those consumers share the same underlying key ring.

6
00:00:30.540 --> 00:00:36.380
A payload protected for one purpose should not be readable by a protector created for a different purpose.

7
00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:42.640
That separation helps prevent one workflow from accidentally accepting payloads created for another workflow.

8
00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:47.880
Purpose names should be specific, stable, and tied to the intended workflow.

9
00:00:47.980 --> 00:00:55.180
A purpose such as a module name plus workflow name and payload type is easier to review than a vague shared string.

10
00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:02.160
The goal is to make it clear why the payload exists, which code should recover it, and whether another consumer should be separate.

11
00:01:02.440 --> 00:01:06.920
Do not reuse the same purpose string for unrelated data types or workflows.

12
00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:17.580
If a confirmation workflow, an export link, and an internal state value all share the same purpose, the application has made review harder and weakened the intended separation.

13
00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.720
Different workflows deserve different protectors.

14
00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.560
Changing purpose strings can break the ability to unprotect existing payloads.

15
00:01:25.660 --> 00:01:31.020
That may invalidate cookies, links, stored state, or account workflow tokens.

16
00:01:31.120 --> 00:01:38.320
Purpose design should be reviewed before production, and changes should be planned as compatibility events rather than casual refactors.
